      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-08T21:12:49Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(multiaxis): a swarf raster line rises off the blade on a rapid under its lying pose\n\nThe base raster leaves every line on a feed straight up Z whose end is\nin air, so the stamp stated it upright: a 90 deg turn off the lying pose\non every raster end, taken through an air link and turned back on the\nnext plunge. stamp_rise::rapid_rise now runs that rise as a rapid holding\nthe pose the line cut with, when the rise is straight up Z, ends in air,\nnever brings the ball nearer the part than it started, and the held\nlean (not under level) has its flank proved in air at every half-radius\nsample. Anything unproved keeps the upright feed and the link law.\n\nA rapid claims no contact, so the verifier's posed-contact law (which\nconvicted #17's posed feed in air) has nothing to read; the posed-rapid\nsolid law judges it like every link retract. Both app blade tests and\ntheir full-band twins verify clean; the pivot5d envelope scan passes.\n\nAir links on the debug blade: swarf 34 -> 23, ladder 53 -> 34 (full\nband 55 -> 44, 86 -> 67); the ratchets in swarf_pose_continuity are\nlowered to those counts (main was red on the ladder's pair, 53 > 51 and\n86 > 84).\n\nFixes #48\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
